<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-Modules" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>What is a Module?</title><!--Chapter 20-->

  <objectives>
    <title>The learning outcomes for this chapter are to...</title>
    <ul><li><p>...understand the definition of a module.</p></li>
      <li><p>...be able to give several examples of modules.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <p>
    The way I think of a module is that it’s exactly like a vector space, except over a ring instead of a field.
    However, if you’re not confident you’ve got the axioms of a vector space right, this poses an issue.
    Informally, the key to vector spaces is that we can add vectors together, and we can scale them by our constants, which come from a field (frequently <m>\R</m> in your past courses as early introductions typically happen in Calculus and Linear Algebra courses).
    Also, things work “nicely” or “as expected.” Here’s a more formal definition of a module.
    If you’d like a formal definition of a vector space, replace the ring below with a field.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="defn-Module">
    <statement>
      <p> Let <m>(R,+_{R},\cdot)</m> be a ring and <m>(M,+_{M})</m> an abelian group.
        We say <m>M</m> is a <alert>left <m>R</m>-module</alert> (or just a <alert>left module</alert> if the ring in question is clear) if there is a function <m>\mu:R\times M\to M</m> typically denoted as <m>\mu(r,m)=rm</m> for all <m>r\in R</m> and <m>m\in M</m> such that the following properties hold: 
        <dl>
        <li><title>(A)</title><p>
            A sort of “associative property” holds.
            For all <m>r,s\in R</m> and <m>m\in M</m>, we have <m>r(sm)=(r\cdot s)m</m>.</p></li>
        <li><title>(D)</title><p>
            Two sort of “distributive properties” hold.
            For all <m>r,s\in R</m> and <m>m,n\in M</m>, we have
            <me>
              (r+_{R}s)m=rm+_{M}sm
            </me>
            and
            <me>
              r(m+_{M}n)=rm+_{M}rn.
            </me></p></li></dl> 
        If <m>R</m> has an identity, denoted <m>1</m>, then we also require that the identity satisfies
        <me>
          1m=m
        </me>
        for all <m>m\in M</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark>
    <p>
      We could also define a <alert>right <m>R</m>-module</alert> with a function <m>\nu:M\times R\to M</m> by insisting instead that <m>m(r\cdot s)=(mr)s</m>, <m>(m+_{M}n)s=ms+_{M}ns</m>, and <m>m(r+_{R}s)=mr+_{M}ms</m>.
      Note that an abelian group could simultaneously be a left <m>R</m>-module and a right <m>S</m>-module.
      As long as these two module structures are compatible, we call this an <alert> <m>R</m>-<m>S</m>-bimodule</alert>.
      I currently have zero intention to discuss bimodules further than this awkward remark.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <remark>
    <p>
      If <m>R</m> is a commutative ring, then every left <m>R</m>-module is automatically a right module as well via <m>\nu(m,r)=rm=\mu(r,m)</m>, and so we drop the left/right distinction and simply refer to <m>R</m>-modules when <m>R</m> is commutative.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Any ring, <m>R</m>, is both a left and right <m>R</m>-module.
        Why?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the (commutative) ring <m>R=\R</m> and abelian group (under addition) <m>M=\R[X]</m>.
        We have that <m>M</m> is an <m>R</m>-module.
        Indeed, for any <m>r\in R</m> and <m>m\in M</m>, we know that <m>r</m> is a real number and <m>m</m> is a polynomial in <m>X</m> with real coefficients.
        The module action <m>\mu:R\times M\to M</m> is given by the multiplication <m>\mu(r,m)=rm</m> where we think of <m>r</m> as a constant polynomial.
        What else needs to be shown?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>R</m> be a ring and <m>I</m> a left ideal of <m>R</m>.
        Then <m>I</m> is a left <m>R</m>-module.
        Is this clear?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>(G,+)</m> be an abelian group.
        Then <m>G</m> is a <m>\Z</m>-module.
        Is this clear?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>R</m> be a commutative ring and <m>I</m> an ideal of <m>R</m>.
        Then <m>R/I</m> is an <m>R</m>-module.
        Indeed, we can define the function <m>\mu</m> via
        <md>
          <mrow>\mu(r,a+I)=ra+I, \text{ i.e.
          }r({a}+I)=(ra)+I\text{ for }r\in R,~a+I\in R/I.</mrow>
        </md>
        Should we check this is well-defined, or is that clear? With this function in place, we can show the required properties: <dl>
        <li>
          <title>(A)</title>
          <p>
            For <m>r,s\in R</m> and <m>a+I\in R/I</m>, we have
            <md>
              <mrow>r(s(a+I))&amp;=r((sa)+I)</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;=r(sa)+I</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;=(rs)a+I</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;=(rs)(a+I).</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>(D)</title>
          <p>
            For <m>r,s\in R</m> and <m>a+I,b+I\in R/I</m>, we have
            <md>
              <mrow>(r+s)(a+I)&amp;=((r+s)a)+I</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;=(ra+sa)+I</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;=(ra+I)+(sa+I)</mrow>
            </md>
            and
            <md>
              <mrow>r((a+I)+(b+I))&amp;=r((a+b)+I)</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;=(r(a+b))+I</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;=(ra+rb)+I</mrow>
              <mrow>&amp;=(ra+I)+(rb+I).</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

<exercises xml:id="exercises-Modules">
  <exercise number="20.A">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Complete the missing parts above.
        They’re listed below to help you out!
      </p>
      <ol marker="(i)">
        <li>
          <p>
            Explain why, for any ring <m>R</m>, <m>R</m> is both a left and right <m>R</m>-module.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Fill in the missing details to show that <m>\R[X]</m> is an <m>\R</m>-module.
            Note that as <m>\R</m> is a field, this should mean that <m>\R[X]</m> is a <m>\R</m>-vector space! What’s the vector space dimension?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Show that for any left ideal <m>I</m> of any ring <m>R</m>, <m>I</m> is a left <m>R</m>-module.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Show that every abelian group <m>G</m> is a <m>\Z</m>-module.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise number="20.B">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Here are some basic properties of modules.
        For each, let <m>R</m> be a ring with identity, and <m>M</m> a left <m>R</m>-module.
      </p>
      <ol marker="(i)">
        <li>
          <p>
            Show that <m>0_Rm=0_M</m> and <m>(-1)m=-m</m> for all <m>m\in M</m>, where <m>-m</m> denotes the inverse of <m>m</m> in <m>M</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Show that <m>r0_M=0_M</m> for all <m>r\in R</m> where <m>0_M</m> denotes the identity element in <m>M</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Assume that <m>rm=0_M</m> for some <m>r\in R</m> and <m>m\in M\setminus\{0\}</m>.
            Prove that <m>r</m> does not have a left inverse, i.e.
            there is no element <m>s\in R</m> such that <m>sr=1</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise number="20.C">
    <statement><p>
      I want to separate out submodules as a separate chapter, and then hopefully do module homomorphisms, so let’s skip this part here!
    </p></statement>
  </exercise>
</exercises>
</chapter>